    "title": "Mogadishu VAT implementation limited to major hotels and businesses, report finds",
    "original_title": "DABAGAL: Waa Sidee Hirgelinta Canshuurta Macaashmacaashka (VAT) ee Muqdisho?",
    "slug": "mogadishu-vat-implementation-limited-to-major-hotels-and-businesses-report-finds",
    "summary": "A Goobjoog investigation reveals that Somalia's 5% VAT is only being collected from large hotels and enterprises in Mogadishu, despite government pledges of broad, equitable enforcement.",
    "content": "The Somali federal government has intensified efforts to implement a 5% value-added tax (VAT) in Mogadishu, presenting it as a key revenue source to fund essential services and reduce reliance on external aid. Officials have repeatedly stated that the tax would be applied uniformly to all businesses and consumers. However, a recent investigation by Goobjoog reporters indicates that actual collection remains confined to a limited number of large hotels and commercial establishments in the capital.\n\nThe VAT, introduced as part of broader fiscal reforms, targets goods and services at the point of sale. Government spokespeople have framed it as a fair measure to broaden the domestic tax base. Yet the disparity between policy and practice raises questions about enforcement capacity and equity. Smaller traders, street vendors, and many informal sector actors appear unaffected, suggesting significant gaps in administrative reach.\n\nThis uneven implementation has particular implications for the Somali diaspora, many of whom run or invest in businesses in Mogadishu. Those operating smaller enterprises may face uncertainty about when or how the tax will be applied to them, while diaspora-owned hotels and large firms are already required to comply. The lack of clear timelines and consistent enforcement complicates business planning and may deter investment.\n\nAnalysts note that effective VAT collection requires robust registration systems, reliable record-keeping, and widespread taxpayer compliance—challenges in an economy where much activity is cash-based. For now, the tax's limited coverage means its revenue impact is far below potential, and the burden falls disproportionately on a small segment of formal businesses.\n\nThe report underscores the need for transparent communication and gradual expansion of the tax net to avoid discouraging formalization and investment. As the government seeks to boost domestic revenue, ensuring fair and predictable enforcement will be critical for maintaining public trust and economic stability.\n\nRead the original report from Goobjoog here: https://goobjoog.com/2026/07/26/dabagal-waa-sidee-hirgelinta-canshuurta-macaashmacaashka-vat-ee-muqdisho/",
